  "explanation": {
    "summary": "To say that X is NOT Y, take the sentence you already know, [Topic] は [Noun] です, and replace です with じゃないです. Nothing else moves: the topic still comes first and is marked with は, the noun still sits in the middle, and the ending still comes last. So 私は 学生です (I am a student) becomes 私は 学生じゃないです (I am not a student). In Japanese, whether a sentence is positive or negative is only decided at the very end, so you have to listen all the way to the last word.",
    "structure_notes": [
      "Slot order: [Topic] + は + [Noun] + じゃないです。 The 'is not' part always comes last, after the noun.",
      "は marks the topic, meaning what we are talking about. It does not change in a negative sentence.",
      "じゃないです works as one block that means 'is not / am not / are not'. It attaches straight to the noun, with no particle in between.",
      "A common way to answer: say 'no' with いいえ, use じゃないです for the wrong idea, then give the right one with です. Example: いいえ、私は 学生じゃないです。かいしゃいんです。",
      "In the second sentence (かいしゃいんです), the topic 私は is left out because everyone already knows who we mean."
    ],
    "connects_to": "This is the negative version of 〜は〜です from episode 1. It is also the natural 'no' answer to the 〜ですか questions from episode 2: if someone asks 学生ですか, you can reply いいえ、学生じゃないです."
  },
